module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.CanonicalSectionRegular
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.ZeroSchemeFactorization

/-!
# The four properties of the zero scheme of a section

Let `X` be an algebraic space over a scheme `S` contained in a big fppf site `B`, let `L` be an
`𝒪_X`-module and let `s` be a global section of `L`.  Stacks 0846 makes four assertions about
the zero scheme `Z(s)` of Stacks 0845: it is the maximal closed subspace on which `s` vanishes;
a morphism of algebraic spaces factors through it exactly when the pulled-back section
vanishes; it is a locally principal closed subspace; and it is an effective Cartier divisor
exactly when `s` is a regular section.

Two kinds of statement are collected here.  The first kind is about the ideal
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt` of `s` on an étale chart `U` where `L` is
free of rank one, and is proved with no hypothesis beyond that freeness: the ideal is generated
by the single coordinate `l₀(s|_U)` of the section, and that generator is a nonzerodivisor
exactly when multiplication by `s|_U` is injective.  These are the chartwise content of parts
(3) and (4) of Stacks 0846.  The second kind is the four assertions themselves, each carrying
as an explicit hypothesis a closed subspace of `X` whose defining ideal is the evaluation ideal
of `s` on the affine étale charts.  **The only Stacks 0846 tag placed here is on
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isRegularSection_iff_forall_injective_mul_generator`**, a statement of
the first kind, which carries no hypothesis beyond the chartwise freeness of `L`; it reads the
second half of part (4) chartwise.  No tag is placed on the four assertions and no part of
Stacks 0846 is claimed for them; see the implementation notes for which input is missing and
why it is needed for each part.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt_eq_span_singleton` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.sectionEvaluationIdealAt_isPrincipal`: on an étale chart where `L` is
  free of rank one, the evaluation ideal of `s` is generated by the coordinate of `s` in that
  basis.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isRegularSection_iff_forall_injective_smul`: a global section of an
  `𝒪_X`-module is regular exactly when multiplication by it is injective on the regular
  functions of every étale chart.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.injective_smul_eval_iff_injective_mul_generator` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isRegularSection_iff_forall_injective_mul_generator`: on charts where
  `L` is free of rank one that injectivity is injectivity of multiplication by the generator of
  the evaluation ideal.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroScheme_isGreatest` is part (1) of Stacks 0846 and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_comp_eq_zeroScheme_iff` is part (2), both with the vanishing
  of the pulled-back section read chartwise.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_zeroScheme_eq` identifies the defining ideal of the
  zero scheme with the evaluation ideal of `s`, and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt_zeroScheme_isPrincipal` is the resulting chartwise
  form of part (3).
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isLocallyPrincipal_zeroScheme_iff` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isEffectiveCartierDivisor_zeroScheme_iff` reduce parts (3) and (4) to
  the corresponding assertions about a closed subspace with a prescribed defining ideal.

## Implementation notes

The hypothesis carried by the last six results is the one of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroSchemeSubfunctor_eq`: a closed subspace `Z ⊆ X` whose defining
ideal on every affine étale chart is the evaluation ideal of `s`.  It is exactly what the
surjectivity half of Stacks 03MB would supply — that a quasi-coherent ideal of `𝒪_X` is the
defining ideal of a closed subspace — and the two obstructions to that half are recorded in the
section "What is not proved" of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ClosedSubspaceIdealBijection.lean`.  Each part of
Stacks 0846 needs it, for the same reason: `Z(s)` is defined as an *intersection* of closed
subspaces whose defining ideals are bounded above by the evaluation ideal, so nothing forces
the defining ideal of the intersection to contain the evaluation ideal, and without that
containment `s` is not known to vanish on `Z(s)`.  The containment fails outright when the
family is `{X}`, where the intersection is `X` itself and its defining ideal is zero.  Thus:
part (1) loses the assertion that `Z(s)` *belongs* to the ordered set it is maximal in — only
the upper bound, `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.le_zeroSchemeSubfunctor`, is unconditional; part (2)
loses the direction factoring implies vanishing — only the converse,
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.exists_comp_eq_zeroScheme_of_sectionEvaluationIdealAt_le`, is
unconditional; and parts (3), (4) are conditions on the defining ideal sheaf of `Z(s)` and so
say nothing at all until that sheaf is identified.

Parts (1) and (2) are stated with the vanishing of the pulled-back section read chartwise, as
in `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ZeroSchemeFactorization.lean`: for part (1) the
containment of the evaluation ideal of `s` in the defining ideal of the closed subspace, and for
part (2) the vanishing of the evaluation ideal after pullback of regular functions along every
point of the source lying over an affine chart.  The equivalence of either reading with the
vanishing of the pulled-back section in the sense of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.modulePullback` is not available.

Parts (3) and (4) are stated only as reductions, not as implications: `IsLocallyPrincipal` and
`IsEffectiveCartierDivisor` of Stacks 083B are conditions on `𝓘_{Z(s)}` *as a sheaf* on the
small étale site, and after the identification of that sheaf there remains the step from the
chartwise statements proved here to a covering on whose members the sheaf is generated by one
section.  That step is local triviality of an invertible module on a site — the descent clause
of Stacks 0B8M — which this library has only for a ringed space whose stalks are local rings,
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isInvertible_iff_isFiniteLocallyFreeOfRank_one`, and the comparison with
chart ideal sheaves that would transport it, Stacks 083C, is likewise unavailable; see the
section "What is not proved" of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/EffectiveCartierEtaleCharts.lean`.

Freeness of `L` on a chart is carried as a generator `t` of `L(U)` together with a linear
functional `l₀` on `L(U)` satisfying `l₀ x • t = x`; no normalisation of `l₀` at `t` is needed
for any of the arguments.  Under it the evaluation ideal, which is by definition the ideal
generated by all the values `l(s|_U)`, collapses to the principal ideal generated by
`l₀(s|_U)`, because `l` is determined on `s|_U` by its value at `t`.

No invertibility hypothesis on `L` appears, following
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.zeroSchemeSubfunctor_isClosedSubspace` and the results of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ZeroSchemeMaximality.lean`, which are also stated for a
section of an arbitrary `𝒪_X`-module.  For an invertible `L` the chart hypothesis above holds
locally, which is what Stacks 0846 uses it for.

